 to have it fall on the head of one of the Calvinists who put it there, causing some serious injuries to the Calvinist and enacting posthumous revenge.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4094.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The final room of the castle has a cross marked on the wall to signal the fact that Philip has gone on a crusade. Philip&#039;s first crusade was in 1177 while he was still married to Elizabeth. His second crusade was in 1190, shortly after marrying his second wife, Matilda of Portugal.\n&lt;p&gt;Some believe that Count Philip went on the crusades to atone for his sins, one of which was killing Elizabeth&#039;s lover in 1175. They believe that he wanted to be clear of his sins in order to be able to father a child. Nevertheless, Philip never made it back from his second crusade, dying of an epidemic that was going through the camp.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3518\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The final room of the castle has a cross marked on the wall to signal the fact that Philip has gone on a crusade. Philip&#039;s first crusade was in 1177 while he was still married to Elizabeth. His second crusade was in 1190, shortly after marrying his second wife, Matilda of Portugal.\n&lt;p&gt;Some believe that Count Philip went on the crusades to atone for his sins, one of which was killing Elizabeth&#039;s lover in 1175. They believe that he wanted to be clear of his sins in order to be able to father a child. Nevertheless, Philip never made it back from his second crusade, dying of an epidemic that was going through the camp.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/upload